[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="EnemyDef" load_steps=2 format=3]

; What the gate was barred against, and the only fight in the game that cannot be fled - by
; anybody in it. Unfleeability is a property of the ENCOUNTER: one boss in a formation refuses
; the escape for the whole of it.
;
; It does not come alone. The record names a Gloom and a Slink beside it, which is Final
; Fantasy I's mixed formation ("a mix of up to 6 small and 2 large") and exactly fills the three
; foes the screen draws - so the boss out-scales the ordinary pairs instead of matching them.
;
; The numbers are still the difficulty statement, and it is now made of a formation against a
; party rather than of a duel. At level 2 a party that times every press clears all three in
; about five rounds with roughly half its health; a party that times NOTHING is wiped in about
; three, against a formation that would have needed ten. Neither outcome depends on luck, which
; is the point: this fight asks whether you learned the timing, and answers honestly. That is
; no longer arithmetic in a comment - test_battle_content PLAYS it, both ways, on twelve seeds.
;
; TWO GLOOMS WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED: it is unwinnable even with every press timed. The
; driver reports it as such on four seeds if anyone tries again.
;
; The two tonics from the smith are what sit between those two extremes, for a party that
; lands some presses and misses others. Gold is 20 rather than 25 so the formation still pays
; the 25 this fight always paid.
;
; IT HAS ITS OWN THEME, which is the one place this game leaves natural A minor - vigil
; leans on G#, and a boss should be the thing that sounds wrong. The field is on the ENEMY
; rather than behind the boss flag because the references disagree about which fights get a
; second battle theme: Dragon Quest I reserves one for the Dragonlord, Final Fantasy I has
; none at all, Final Fantasy IV plays one for nearly every boss. In a formation the first
; foe that states a track wins, so his escort does not have to be silent about it.

; RESISTS FIRE (50%), so Ember lands 3 on it rather than 7. The point is that the trick which
; deletes its escort does not delete IT - burn the Gloom, then swing. A boss that shrugged off
; nothing would make the whole fight one repeated cast, and a boss immune to fire would make
; the spell dead weight in the encounter it matters most in; half is the answer that keeps the
; cast legal and stops it being the only plan.
;
; Its formation is where the whole system is visible at once: the same Ember is doubled
; against the Gloom standing beside it and halved against this. UNJUDGED BY EAR.

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://scripts/data/enemy_def.gd" id="1_enemy"]

